course: books
lesson: 88
title: Just So Stories, How the Alphabet Was Made, part 2: yo, so, sho, shi, and shu-ya, the sky-water
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tegumai shows Taffy the big secret: put two sound-signs side by side and you get a word. A carp-tail and a round egg make «yo», bad swamp-water; scratched beside a pool, it would warn Taffy «don't drink this» even if Daddy were miles away hunting.
explain: A snake and an egg make «so», food cooked on the fire, which means dinner is ready. But «sho» means the drying-poles, and Taffy hates hanging the hot, heavy, hairy hides. If she mixed up so and sho, she would come running for dinner and be put to work! So they make a new sign for the sh-sound: a picture of the drying-poles themselves.
explain: Daddy jokes with «shi», the word for spear, and draws it as one straight line: «Even Mummy couldn't mistake that for me being killed.» Taffy asks him not to tease. For «shu», sky, they need a thin oo-sound, so Daddy draws the round egg with a little hole at the end, where the sound runs out thin.
explain: Then Daddy writes a whole word and tells Taffy not to look up. She reads it sign by sign: «Shu-ya. Sky-water (rain).» Just then a real raindrop falls on her hand. Daddy told her it would rain without saying a word, and they both dance for joy.
fact: In Tegumai's language «ya» means water, and river too, as in «Wagai-ya»; «yo» is bad, black swamp-water that gives you fever.
fact: Sign (8.), which Tegumai scratches, is carp-tail plus round egg = yo. Taffy: «Two noises mixed! Yo, bad water.»
fact: Taffy sees the point: it is «just the same as if you stood there and said, "G'way, Taffy, or you'll get fever."»
fact: «So» (snake and egg) is food cooked on the fire, «dinner's ready»; «sho» means the drying-poles where they hang the hides.
fact: Tegumai first thinks of «a spotty snake that hisses sh-sh», but Taffy fears the spots could be left out in a hurry, so for sh they put a picture of the drying-poles themselves «just after the hissy-snake», «to make quite sure».
fact: «Shi» is Tegumai for spear; he draws it as a plain straight line, «We won't have beavers or hills this time».
fact: «Shu» means sky; its end sound is «the round-egg-sound made thin». Taffy suggests a thin egg, «a frog that hasn't eaten anything for years», but Daddy fears it would be mistaken for the round egg.
fact: So Daddy opens «a little hole at the end of the round egg to show how the O-noise runs out all thin, ooo-oo-oo».
fact: Taffy reads (13.) sign by sign: «Snake, pole, broken egg, carp-tail and carp-mouth ... Shu-ya. Sky-water (rain).»
fact: Taffy says that if she saw «shu-ya» scratched on the wall in the morning, she would know to take her beaver-skin hood.
fact: «Daddies didn't mind doing those things in those days», says Kipling, when Tegumai gets up and dances.
fact: Real life: using pictures for their SOUNDS, not their meaning, is called the rebus idea; a picture of an eye and a saw can spell «I saw». The first writers in Egypt and Mesopotamia used it too.
fact: Real life: with a small set of sound-signs you can write ANY word, even words for things you cannot draw, like «ending» or «come». That is why an alphabet is so powerful: English needs only 26 letters, but Chinese writing uses thousands of characters.
fact: Real life: rain is water that evaporated from seas and land, rose, cooled into clouds and fell again; «sky-water» is a good name for it.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «mid» = only so-so, not good; «gurt» (West Country) = great, big; «dreich» (Scotland) = grey, wet, gloomy weather.
exercise: What is «ya» in the Tegumai language? | Water, and river too | Fire, and the smoke from it | Fish, especially carp | Sky, and the clouds in it
exercise: What is «yo»? | Bad black swamp-water that gives you fever | Good clear spring-water from the back of the Cave, safe for drinking | A carp-fish | Yes
exercise: Which two signs make «yo»? | Carp-tail and round egg | Carp-mouth and snake | Snake and egg | Egg and drying-poles
exercise: Why is a «yo» sign by a pool useful? | It warns «don't drink» even when Daddy is miles away | It tells you where the best fish are hiding in the swamp | It shows a beaver lives there | It means swim here
exercise: What does «so» mean? | Food cooked on the fire, dinner's ready | The drying-poles where the heavy hides hang | A spear | Sky
exercise: What does «sho» mean? | The drying-poles where they hang the hides | Dinner is ready, so come home to the Cave at once | Bad water | A snake
exercise: Why does Taffy hate the drying-poles? | She has to help hang heavy, hot, hairy hides on them | They are full of big spiders that drop into her hair when she passes | They fell on her once | They block the Cave
exercise: Why must so and sho have different signs? | Taffy might come for dinner and be put to work on the hides instead | So and sho mean exactly the same thing in Tegumai talk | Mummy can't read snakes | Snakes are not allowed
exercise: What was Tegumai's first idea for the sh-sound? | A spotty snake that hisses sh-sh | A drying-pole standing all by itself | A mouth with a finger pressed on it | A shell
exercise: Why does Taffy say no to the spotty snake? | Daddy might leave the spots out in a hurry, and she'd be caught | She cannot draw spots, because her shark's tooth always slips on the bark | Spots mean danger | Snakes don't have spots
exercise: How do they write the sh-sound in the end? | The snake with a picture of the drying-poles just after it | A spotty snake, drawn very carefully with the spots in | A carp's tail | A broken egg
exercise: What does «shi» mean? | Spear | Sky | Fish | Shoe
exercise: How does Tegumai draw his spear this time? | As one straight line | With beavers and hills | Sticking in his back | With a black handle and shark's teeth all along it
exercise: Why does Taffy ask Daddy not to make fun of her? | She remembers her picture-letter and the mud | He laughs at her drawing of the carp's mouth and says it looks like a frog | He calls her small | He takes her shark's tooth
exercise: What does «shu» mean? | Sky | Shoe | Snake | Ship
exercise: The end of «shu» is «the round-egg-sound made thin». What does Taffy first suggest? | A thin egg, like a starving frog | A long stick lying on its side | A small round egg, half the size | A broken stone
exercise: Why does Daddy refuse the thin egg? | It might be mistaken for the round egg itself | Frogs are much too hard to draw well on birch-bark with a tooth | It is too thin to see | Taffy drew it badly
exercise: What does Daddy draw for the thin oo-sound? | The round egg with a little hole | A thin snake lying flat, hissing very quietly | A tiny carp, drawn with its mouth open | Two round eggs, one inside the other
exercise: What word does Taffy read without looking up? | Shu-ya, sky-water, rain | Ya-las, water is running out | So, dinner is ready at the Cave | Yo, bad water, don't drink it
exercise: What happens just as she reads it? | A raindrop falls on her hand | A carp jumps | Mummy calls from the Cave | Thunder rumbles over the hills and she runs for shelter
exercise: What does Taffy say she would do if she saw «shu-ya» on the wall in the morning? | Take her beaver-skin hood | Stay in bed all day long | Go fishing | Fill the water-tank
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what sign stood for the ssh-sound? | A snake | A round egg | A carp-mouth | A carp-tail
exercise: Looking back: in part 1, what did a snake by the back-cave door mean? | Daddy is thinking, so come in quietly | Danger, there is a real snake in there, so run away | Dinner is ready | Gone fishing
test: Finn: «Taffy guessed shu-ya cos she saw it raining, that's mid.» (mid = so-so) Is that fair? | No: she read it sign by sign first, and the raindrop only made her sure | Yes, she couldn't read any of it and just guessed from the weather | Yes, Daddy told her | No, she read it in the smoke
test: Why is writing «yo» better than Taffy's picture-letter way? | It gives the exact word, so it can't be read as something else | It is prettier and takes much longer to draw, so people look at it carefully | It uses more beavers | It needs a Stranger-man
test: What would happen if they used the plain snake for both s and sh? | «Dinner» and «drying-poles» would look the same | Nothing would change at all, because nobody would ever mix them up | Snakes would be cross | Taffy would get more dinners
test: Taffy says a thin egg would be «a frog that hasn't eaten anything for years». Why is it a joke? | A starving frog is a silly picture for a sound | Real frogs never eat anything at all, not even flies, for years and years | Frogs say oo | Eggs turn into frogs
test: Which is TRUE about this part? | Tegumai tells Taffy about the rain without saying a word | Taffy draws «shu-ya» herself and Daddy reads it aloud, sign by sign | They tell Mummy straight away | It is sunny all day
test: Real life: what is the «rebus» idea? | Using a picture for its sound, like an eye and a saw for «I saw» | Using a picture for exactly what it shows and nothing else, like a cow for a cow | Writing only numbers | Drawing maps
test: Real life: why is an alphabet so powerful? | A few sound-signs can write any word at all | It needs one picture for every single word, so nothing is ever mixed up | It can only write names | It works only for fish
test: Real life: how many letters does English need, and how many characters does Chinese writing use? | 26 letters; thousands of characters | Thousands of letters; 26 characters | 26 letters; 26 characters as well | Ten letters; a hundred characters
test: Real life, on a dreich (grey, wet) day: where does «sky-water» come from? | Water evaporates, makes clouds, and falls again | Rain is made by a carp in the sky | The Moon spills it | It comes from inside the mountains
test: Looking back: the Tribe thought Taffy's picture showed Tegumai being speared. What does Tegumai joke here? | «Even Mummy couldn't mistake that for me being killed» | «Let's draw beavers and hills again, they were such a great success with the Tribe» | «Mummy will be so pleased» | «Send the Stranger-man»
test: Looking back: what did Tegumai pay the Stranger-man for the mud? | Two soft deerskins with fringes | A spear | Nothing | A carp
test: Looking back: why did Tegumai draw a feeler on the carp-mouth? | So it would not be mistaken for a perch or trout | To make it prettier, so Mummy would like it on the wall | Because Taffy asked | To show it was angry
test: Looking back: which earlier character said one word, «Humph!», and got something on his back for it? | The Camel | The Kangaroo | The Rhinoceros | The Tortoise
test: Jake from Bristol: «gurt idea, Tegumai, draw a spear as a whole battle scene.» (gurt = great) What does he actually do? | Draws one straight line | Draws a battle, with beavers, hills and the whole Tribe | Draws only the black handle | Draws Mummy holding the spear
test: ★★★ Taffy reads «snake, pole, broken egg, carp-tail and carp-mouth». How many signs is that, and how many sounds does «shu-ya» have? | Five signs: snake and pole together for sh, then oo, y and ah | Four signs; four sounds | Two signs; two sounds | Six signs; three sounds
test: ★★★ Using only the signs from this lesson (carp-mouth ah, carp-tail y, egg o, snake s, drying-poles sh, thin egg oo), which of these could they NOT write yet? | «Maru» | «Yo» | «So» | «Shu-ya»
test: ★★★ Careful: «S'pose you saw this scratched by the side of a pool». Who drew the «yo» sign (8.)? | Tegumai | Taffy | Teshumai | The Stranger-man
exam: Identify the meaning, in the language of the Tribe of Tegumai, of the word «yo». | Bad water, such as that of a swamp, which occasions fever | Food cooked upon the fire | The sky | A spear
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a separate sign was adopted for the sound sh. | Lest «so» be confounded with «sho» | Because the Head Chief had prohibited the use of snakes in any sign whatsoever | Because Teshumai was unable to discern spots | Because the sh-sound occurs but once
exam: It is given that Tegumai required a sign for the thin oo-sound. Select the response most consistent with his solution. | A round egg with a small aperture at one end | A thin egg resembling a starving frog | A second snake facing backwards | A line beneath the egg
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the advantage of an alphabet over picture-writing? | A small number of sound-signs suffices to write any word whatsoever | It requires a separate picture for every object | It may be read only aloud | It cannot record words for which no picture exists
